Selecting a Storage Class for an Application
Objective
Choose the correct StorageClass so that an application gets the right type of storage.
Storage differs in:
•	Performance
•	Cost
•	Reliability
•	Access type
•	Backup policies
Developers choose a StorageClass instead of dealing with physical storage directly.
________________________________________
1. What is a StorageClass?
A StorageClass defines how storage should be provided in Kubernetes/OpenShift.
It describes:
•	Storage provider
•	Performance type
•	Provisioning method
•	Reclaim behavior
•	Volume expansion support
Simple Architecture
Application
     ↓
PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)
     ↓
StorageClass
     ↓
Provisioner
     ↓
PersistentVolume (PV)
     ↓
Actual Storage (NFS, AWS EBS, etc)
________________________________________
2. Why StorageClasses Exist
Clusters can have multiple storage systems.
Examples:
Storage	Use Case
SSD	high performance
HDD	cheap storage
NFS	shared storage
Cloud disk	persistent cloud storage
Administrators create StorageClasses to represent these.
Example:
fast-ssd
standard-hdd
backup-storage
dev-storage
Developers simply choose the correct class.
________________________________________
3. Default StorageClass
Clusters normally have a default StorageClass.
If a PVC does not specify one, Kubernetes automatically uses the default.
Example annotation:
storageclass.kubernetes.io/is-default-class: "true"
Important rule:
Even if default exists, developers should explicitly specify StorageClass to avoid problems if the default changes.
________________________________________
4. Reclaim Policy
Reclaim policy defines what happens when a PVC is deleted.
Two main policies exist.
________________________________________
4.1 Delete Policy (Default)
When PVC is deleted:
PVC → Deleted
PV → Deleted
Actual Storage → Deleted
Data → Lost
Advantages
•	Automatic cleanup
•	Lower storage cost
Example use:
temporary application data
test environments
________________________________________
4.2 Retain Policy
When PVC is deleted:
PVC → Deleted
PV → Kept
Storage → Kept
Data → Kept
Administrator must manually clean storage.
Advantages:
Data is preserved
Use cases:
databases
critical data
production storage
________________________________________
5. Manual Reclaim Steps (Retain Policy)
If reclaim policy = Retain
Administrator must manually reclaim storage.
Step 1
Delete the PV
oc delete pv pv-name
This removes PV from Kubernetes.
________________________________________
Step 2
Storage still exists in external infrastructure:
Examples:
•	AWS EBS
•	Azure Disk
•	Google Persistent Disk
•	OpenStack Cinder
Admin can:
Option 1 – Reuse the storage
Option 2 – Delete storage manually
________________________________________
6. Kubernetes Storage Responsibility
Important rule:
Kubernetes does NOT protect applications from bad storage usage.
Applications must handle:
•	data consistency
•	locking
•	integrity
Example problem:
Two databases sharing the same disk.
DB1 → writes data
DB2 → overwrites data
Kubernetes does not prevent this.
________________________________________
7. Access Modes
PVCs specify how storage can be accessed.
ReadWriteOnce (RWO)
Only ONE node can mount the volume
Common for:
databases
Example:
iSCSI
cloud disks
________________________________________
ReadWriteMany (RWX)
Multiple nodes can read/write
Example:
NFS
shared storage
________________________________________
Important note:
Access mode does not guarantee safe sharing.
Even with RWX:
Applications can overwrite each other's data
________________________________________
8. StorageClass Use Cases
Admins design storage classes based on many factors.
________________________________________
8.1 Volume Mode
Two types:
Filesystem
Most common.
Used when applications work with files.
Example:
web servers
shared folders
________________________________________
Block Mode
Provides raw block device.
Benefits:
Higher performance
Used by:
databases
high-performance apps
________________________________________
9. Quality of Service (QoS)
Storage classes can represent performance levels.
Example:
Storage	Performance
SSD	fast
HDD	slow
NVMe	very fast
Developers choose based on application needs.
________________________________________
10. Administrative Tier
Admins may create tiers.
Example:
Tier	Features
Production	backups enabled
Development	cheaper storage
Archive	slow but cheap
________________________________________
11. PVC Matching Process
When PVC is created, Kubernetes searches for a matching PV.
Matching conditions:
1️⃣ Access mode must match
RWO
RWX
2️⃣ Storage size must be enough
PVC request = 10Gi
PV must ≥ 10Gi
3️⃣ StorageClass must match
If no match exists:
PVC status = Pending
________________________________________
12. Requesting a Specific StorageClass
Developers specify it inside PVC.
Example:
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: my-block-pvc
spec:
  accessModes:
    - RWO
  volumeMode: Block
  storageClassName: storage-class-name
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi
Important field:
storageClassName
Only PVs with this StorageClass can bind.
________________________________________
13. StorageClass YAML Example
Example StorageClass:
apiVersion: storage.k8s.io/v1
kind: StorageClass
metadata:
  name: io1-gold-storage
provisioner: ebs.csi.aws.com
reclaimPolicy: Delete
volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
allowVolumeExpansion: true
parameters:
  type: io1
  iopsPerGB: "10"
Explanation:
Field	Meaning
apiVersion	API version
kind	resource type
name	storage class name
provisioner	storage driver
reclaimPolicy	delete or retain
volumeBindingMode	when PV created
allowVolumeExpansion	allow resizing
parameters	storage options
________________________________________
14. Provisioner
Provisioner identifies the storage driver.
Examples:
Provisioner	Storage
kubernetes.io/aws-ebs	AWS EBS
kubernetes.io/gce-pd	Google Disk
topolvm.io	TopoLVM
nfs-subdir-external-provisioner	NFS
Provisioner dynamically creates PVs.
________________________________________
15. VolumeBindingMode
Controls when PV is created.
________________________________________
Immediate (Default)
PV created when PVC is created.
Problem:
Pod scheduling not considered
This may create unusable volumes.
________________________________________
WaitForFirstConsumer
PV created only when a pod uses the PVC.
Benefits:
Better scheduling
Topology-aware storage
Recommended for many environments.
________________________________________
16. Allow Volume Expansion
If enabled:
allowVolumeExpansion: true
Developers can increase PVC size.
Example:
10Gi → 20Gi
Important:
Shrinking volumes is NOT supported
________________________________________
17. Creating StorageClass
Cluster administrator creates it.
Command:
oc create -f storage-class.yaml
StorageClasses are:
cluster-wide
not namespaced
Available to all projects.
________________________________________
18. Viewing StorageClasses
List all storage classes:
oc get storageclass
Example output:
NAME           PROVISIONER
nfs-storage    k8s-sigs.io/nfs-subdir-external-provisioner
lvms-vg1       topolvm.io
________________________________________
19. Inspect StorageClass
Command:
oc describe storageclass lvms-vg1
Example output:
Provisioner: topolvm.io
ReclaimPolicy: Delete
VolumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer
AllowVolumeExpansion: True
Developers use this info to choose storage.
________________________________________
20. Attaching PVC to Deployment
Example command:
oc set volume deployment/myapp \
--add \
--name my-volume \
--claim-name my-block-pvc \
--mount-path /var/tmp
This mounts PVC inside container.
